WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.430
YUFENG GUO: In recent
years, new research

00:00:02.430 --> 00:00:05.220
into understanding how
neural networks work

00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:07.530
has really revolutionized
our ability

00:00:07.530 --> 00:00:10.410
to interpret how they
operate, providing the tools

00:00:10.410 --> 00:00:13.260
and building blocks for
explaining why they make

00:00:13.260 --> 00:00:15.420
the predictions that they do.

00:00:15.420 --> 00:00:18.990
Stay tuned, as we dive into
convolutional neural networks

00:00:18.990 --> 00:00:22.450
and try to understand
how they see the world.

00:00:22.450 --> 00:00:23.890
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:30.610 --> 00:00:32.250
Welcome to "AI
Adventures," where

00:00:32.250 --> 00:00:36.250
we explore the art, science,
and tools of machine learning.

00:00:36.250 --> 00:00:38.710
My name is Yufeng Guo,
and on this episode,

00:00:38.710 --> 00:00:42.480
we're going to see how to use
Lucid to better understand

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:45.870
what neural networks
are looking for.

00:00:45.870 --> 00:00:49.410
Convolutional neural
networks, or CNNs,

00:00:49.410 --> 00:00:51.720
have significantly
advanced the state

00:00:51.720 --> 00:00:53.640
of the art of image recognition.

00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:55.470
They've achieved
incredible accuracy

00:00:55.470 --> 00:00:58.440
across a wide variety of tasks.

00:00:58.440 --> 00:01:00.840
But understanding
what's happening

00:01:00.840 --> 00:01:03.180
between the inputs
and the outputs

00:01:03.180 --> 00:01:06.900
in those hidden layers,
that's been slow going.

00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:09.750
So today, we'll look at a
number of different approaches

00:01:09.750 --> 00:01:12.360
to try to gain some
intuition behind what

00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:15.570
it is that makes a CNN
work, ranging from focusing

00:01:15.570 --> 00:01:18.060
on individual neurons,
all the way to looking

00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:20.430
at the response of
an entire layer.

00:01:20.430 --> 00:01:22.470
Before we dive in,
I want to get us

00:01:22.470 --> 00:01:24.330
all on the same
page about why it

00:01:24.330 --> 00:01:27.350
is that we care about how
a neural network arrives

00:01:27.350 --> 00:01:29.400
at the prediction that it does.

00:01:29.400 --> 00:01:32.260
Isn't it enough that the
final prediction is right?

00:01:32.260 --> 00:01:33.450
Well, not quite.

00:01:33.450 --> 00:01:35.550
In real world,
production use cases

00:01:35.550 --> 00:01:38.310
of machine learning image
recognition systems,

00:01:38.310 --> 00:01:41.790
a lot of additional value can be
gained by understanding the how

00:01:41.790 --> 00:01:44.370
and the why of a
given prediction.

00:01:44.370 --> 00:01:46.740
This helps the end
user of the predictions

00:01:46.740 --> 00:01:48.510
better understand
whether it's being

00:01:48.510 --> 00:01:51.060
made for the right reasons.

00:01:51.060 --> 00:01:53.220
Let's take a
hypothetical example,

00:01:53.220 --> 00:01:57.030
say, in medical imaging,
where small details can really

00:01:57.030 --> 00:01:59.460
play a crucial role
in differentiating

00:01:59.460 --> 00:02:02.310
between different
types of results.

00:02:02.310 --> 00:02:04.470
What if, let's say,
a model not only

00:02:04.470 --> 00:02:07.120
gave just the right
prediction for an image,

00:02:07.120 --> 00:02:11.039
but it also provided information
about which parts of that image

00:02:11.039 --> 00:02:14.790
were the primary contributors
that led to its conclusions?

00:02:14.790 --> 00:02:17.160
Then, a doctor could
see whether this model

00:02:17.160 --> 00:02:21.960
was coming to these conclusions
for the expected reasons.

00:02:21.960 --> 00:02:24.840
Moreover, this approach could
even potentially highlight

00:02:24.840 --> 00:02:27.780
new patterns, which
hadn't yet been identified

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:31.030
as useful in these images.

00:02:31.030 --> 00:02:32.760
So what kinds of
tools and approaches

00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:34.740
would we need in
order to construct

00:02:34.740 --> 00:02:39.420
this kind of understanding of
an image model's predictions?

00:02:39.420 --> 00:02:41.430
Well, let's set
the stage by first

00:02:41.430 --> 00:02:45.210
understanding the structure of
a convolutional neural network.

00:02:45.210 --> 00:02:47.370
Convolutional neural
networks get their name

00:02:47.370 --> 00:02:50.220
from their many
convolutional layers.

00:02:50.220 --> 00:02:53.040
And they work in
concert to understand

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:55.260
the different
details of an image.

00:02:55.260 --> 00:02:57.900
In the early layers
close to the inputs,

00:02:57.900 --> 00:02:59.550
the convolutional
layers are expected

00:02:59.550 --> 00:03:03.930
to be looking for your basic
lines, and simple shapes,

00:03:03.930 --> 00:03:04.860
and patterns.

00:03:04.860 --> 00:03:07.080
Further along in the
network, the work

00:03:07.080 --> 00:03:09.780
from these early layers
are passed onwards

00:03:09.780 --> 00:03:12.600
and respond to more
understandable-looking inputs.

00:03:12.600 --> 00:03:15.510
We might see images that
appear similar to various parts

00:03:15.510 --> 00:03:18.330
of real world
animals or objects.

00:03:18.330 --> 00:03:20.978
This information then
continues propagating onwards

00:03:20.978 --> 00:03:22.770
through the network,
and eventually reaches

00:03:22.770 --> 00:03:25.410
those final layers,
which are put together

00:03:25.410 --> 00:03:29.862
in generating the final outputs
of the network's conclusions.

00:03:29.862 --> 00:03:31.320
These tend to, of
course, look more

00:03:31.320 --> 00:03:33.240
directly like the
categories that we

00:03:33.240 --> 00:03:36.140
are expecting as the outputs.

00:03:36.140 --> 00:03:38.030
There are a number
of parts that make up

00:03:38.030 --> 00:03:40.220
any single convolutional layer.

00:03:40.220 --> 00:03:43.560
At the lowest level is the
single individual neuron.

00:03:43.560 --> 00:03:45.560
Neurons are the most
fundamental building blocks

00:03:45.560 --> 00:03:47.750
of a neural network,
and the strength

00:03:47.750 --> 00:03:50.570
of each neuron's
response to input

00:03:50.570 --> 00:03:54.450
is what we will use to
understand their behavior.

00:03:54.450 --> 00:03:56.300
So now, let's zoom
out a bit and see

00:03:56.300 --> 00:03:58.370
how multiple neurons
can be connected

00:03:58.370 --> 00:04:01.230
in a specific channel.

00:04:01.230 --> 00:04:04.250
So a single channel is made up
of a two-dimensional rectangle

00:04:04.250 --> 00:04:07.970
of neurons, which are used to
process the previous layer's

00:04:07.970 --> 00:04:08.930
outputs.

00:04:08.930 --> 00:04:10.580
There are many
channels in a layer,

00:04:10.580 --> 00:04:13.350
each channel stacked on top of
the other-- much like a layer

00:04:13.350 --> 00:04:14.080
cake.

00:04:14.080 --> 00:04:17.060
And within a whole
layer, all the channels

00:04:17.060 --> 00:04:20.850
receive the same values as
inputs from the previous layer.

00:04:20.850 --> 00:04:24.170
But they each process that
input slightly differently,

00:04:24.170 --> 00:04:26.028
looking for different features.

00:04:26.028 --> 00:04:28.070
All of these outputs are
then combined and passed

00:04:28.070 --> 00:04:31.040
onwards to the next layer.

00:04:31.040 --> 00:04:32.810
Now that we have a
basic understanding

00:04:32.810 --> 00:04:36.660
of how a convolutional neural
network's layers are assembled,

00:04:36.660 --> 00:04:38.450
let's take a look
at how to find out

00:04:38.450 --> 00:04:42.170
what a given neuron, or
perhaps a group of neurons,

00:04:42.170 --> 00:04:44.840
is quote unquote "looking for."

00:04:44.840 --> 00:04:47.060
When an image is passed
through the network in what

00:04:47.060 --> 00:04:51.350
is called a forward pass, each
neuron responds or activates

00:04:51.350 --> 00:04:52.940
to a different degree.

00:04:52.940 --> 00:04:55.730
Now, we can measure that
activation's strength-- well,

00:04:55.730 --> 00:04:58.580
because it's literally just
the number that comes out.

00:04:58.580 --> 00:05:00.290
The greater the
magnitude of that number,

00:05:00.290 --> 00:05:02.520
the stronger the activation.

00:05:02.520 --> 00:05:05.430
Now, these activation values
can be positive or negative.

00:05:05.430 --> 00:05:08.330
So we can have a strong
positive activation, as well as

00:05:08.330 --> 00:05:10.530
strong negative activations.

00:05:10.530 --> 00:05:13.970
So let's start by seeing what a
specific neuron in our network

00:05:13.970 --> 00:05:16.010
responds most
strongly to by looking

00:05:16.010 --> 00:05:19.190
at how it activates when
the network is presented

00:05:19.190 --> 00:05:21.530
with an image input.

00:05:21.530 --> 00:05:25.280
If we take this train model,
and then hold all the weights

00:05:25.280 --> 00:05:26.880
static-- so they can't change--

00:05:26.880 --> 00:05:30.060
and then, we run an image
through that network.

00:05:30.060 --> 00:05:33.500
And we measure what a particular
neuron's activation value

00:05:33.500 --> 00:05:37.580
is, we can begin to
optimize this input.

00:05:37.580 --> 00:05:41.112
Initially, we'll start with
an image of pure static noise.

00:05:41.112 --> 00:05:43.070
And because neural networks
are differentiable,

00:05:43.070 --> 00:05:46.280
we can figure out how to adjust
that input image, that noise--

00:05:46.280 --> 00:05:47.720
there's just nothing there--

00:05:47.720 --> 00:05:51.080
to get an even higher activation
value for that specific neuron

00:05:51.080 --> 00:05:52.260
we're interested in.

00:05:52.260 --> 00:05:54.150
So we tweak that
image a little bit.

00:05:54.150 --> 00:05:56.960
And if we're doing it right,
the activation of the neuron

00:05:56.960 --> 00:05:59.600
will be even higher
when we pass that image

00:05:59.600 --> 00:06:00.840
through the second time.

00:06:00.840 --> 00:06:02.680
So then, we can do it again.

00:06:02.680 --> 00:06:05.350
We can adjust the image
to boost that activation

00:06:05.350 --> 00:06:08.470
of the neurons response to
the image and pass it through.

00:06:08.470 --> 00:06:11.200
Over and over again,
we repeat this process.

00:06:11.200 --> 00:06:13.420
And eventually, you
end up with an image

00:06:13.420 --> 00:06:17.110
that has been optimized to
activate one particular neuron

00:06:17.110 --> 00:06:19.630
of the network maximally.

00:06:19.630 --> 00:06:23.140
The result is an image with
a focused region surrounded

00:06:23.140 --> 00:06:25.250
by kind of a more
blended background.

00:06:25.250 --> 00:06:27.430
Now, these patterns can
be all over the place.

00:06:27.430 --> 00:06:31.330
And thankfully, Lucid has made
this really easy to impute.

00:06:31.330 --> 00:06:33.670
There's no need to write an
optimization loop yourself.

00:06:33.670 --> 00:06:36.820
You can just choose what
layer, channel, and neuron

00:06:36.820 --> 00:06:38.002
you're interested in.

00:06:38.002 --> 00:06:40.420
And it'll take care of the rest.

00:06:40.420 --> 00:06:43.080
Now if we instead look to
optimize the activation of not

00:06:43.080 --> 00:06:47.220
one single neuron, but instead
that entire channel of neurons,

00:06:47.220 --> 00:06:50.370
we can get a pattern that
is a bit more consistent

00:06:50.370 --> 00:06:52.230
and fills in the edges too.

00:06:52.230 --> 00:06:54.870
And this is what we'll
be primarily seeing

00:06:54.870 --> 00:06:57.360
throughout the rest
of our experiments.

00:06:57.360 --> 00:06:59.010
Now, let's take a
look at some ways

00:06:59.010 --> 00:07:01.330
to combine the different
neurons together,

00:07:01.330 --> 00:07:04.630
allowing us to see how
neurons activate in pairs.

00:07:04.630 --> 00:07:07.050
This is showing the
optimized input images

00:07:07.050 --> 00:07:09.720
when optimizing for two
different channels of neurons

00:07:09.720 --> 00:07:12.960
simultaneously,
rather than just one.

00:07:12.960 --> 00:07:15.630
What ends up happening is
that the images show up

00:07:15.630 --> 00:07:19.470
as a blend of the two channels
that they originated from.

00:07:19.470 --> 00:07:21.720
And finally, let's see
how we can interpolate

00:07:21.720 --> 00:07:23.160
between two different neurons.

00:07:23.160 --> 00:07:25.740
Rather than just optimizing
for both equally,

00:07:25.740 --> 00:07:27.690
we can weigh them
unevenly, giving us

00:07:27.690 --> 00:07:30.450
images that are along the
spectrum between the two

00:07:30.450 --> 00:07:31.530
channels.

00:07:31.530 --> 00:07:35.340
This gives us even more context
about what sorts of patterns

00:07:35.340 --> 00:07:37.960
these channels are
able to detect.

00:07:37.960 --> 00:07:41.400
So far, we've gotten
some background on CNNs.

00:07:41.400 --> 00:07:43.830
And we've seen some examples
of feature activation

00:07:43.830 --> 00:07:46.500
at the neuron and channel level.

00:07:46.500 --> 00:07:48.450
In the next episode
of "AI Adventures,"

00:07:48.450 --> 00:07:51.060
we'll take these ideas
of feature visualization

00:07:51.060 --> 00:07:53.490
and apply them to
the entire image

00:07:53.490 --> 00:07:55.650
to create activation grids.

00:07:55.650 --> 00:08:00.270
And then, we can use that to
produce an activation atlas--

00:08:00.270 --> 00:08:04.350
which will give us insight not
to one neuron, channel, layer,

00:08:04.350 --> 00:08:05.410
or just an image.

00:08:05.410 --> 00:08:08.370
But it'll allow us to see how
the entirety of the network

00:08:08.370 --> 00:08:11.580
operates as one cohesive unit.

00:08:11.580 --> 00:08:14.100
If you want to learn more
about feature activations

00:08:14.100 --> 00:08:16.950
and to experiment with
Lucid on your own,

00:08:16.950 --> 00:08:19.230
check out this
Distilled.pub article--

00:08:19.230 --> 00:08:20.460
really fantastic.

00:08:20.460 --> 00:08:23.143
And try out the Lucid
Library on GitHub.

00:08:23.143 --> 00:08:25.560
Thanks for watching this episode
of "Cloud AI Adventures."

